n is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- Its range is $y\\in (0,\\infty )$\n- It is a decreasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bbffed32708d30355ed3592deae845d1a84cd1f1772ef1d0fae7ac39518295.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true."}]}
